				<div class="x-element k-11d23a52 w w-heading">
					<h2 class="x-heading-title x-size-default">Why hair salon marketing <span class="hl">isn't the same as marketing "a beauty salon"</span></h2>				</div>
		<div class="x-element k-56405e15 c-con-full c-flex c-con c-child">
		<div class="x-element k-75114e5c c-con-full c-flex c-con c-child">
				<div class="x-element k-42b4592c w w-text-editor">
									<p>A specialist hair salon is built around colour, chemical treatments and precision cutting. It sells differently from a general multi-service salon. The client isn&#8217;t choosing a location. They&#8217;re choosing a stylist, based on transformations they&#8217;ve already seen. That&#8217;s why SEO for hair salons needs stylist pages, treatment pages and a strong Google profile, not one generic page.</p>								</div>
				<div class="x-element k-73252ee6 w w-heading">
					<h3 class="x-heading-title x-size-default">Instagram marketing for hair salons starts with the stylist's page</h3>				</div>
				<div class="x-element k-6e40f21e w w-text-editor">
									<p>By the time a color client calls or messages, they&#8217;ve usually already picked which stylist they want. Marketing that pushes &#8220;the salon&#8221; as a brand, without giving each stylist their own visible portfolio and following, is marketing the wrong thing.</p>								</div>
				<div class="x-element k-71293fb6 w w-heading">
					<h3 class="x-heading-title x-size-default">Hair salon rebooking cycles vary by service</h3>				</div>
				<div class="x-element k-1c101e80 w w-text-editor">
									<p>A cut-and-blow-dry client rebooks every 4-6 weeks; a full-color client rebooks every 6-8 weeks; a keratin client rebooks every 3-5 months. A single generic &#8220;come back soon&#8221; campaign misses all three cycles at once — each needs its own timed prompt.</p>								</div>
